{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-07-ratios-fractions-and-percentage-comparisons",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Ratios, Fractions, and Percentage Comparisons",
    "description": "Move between ratios, fractions of a whole, and percent comparisons without changing the base.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the ratio model from the quantities named, explain why each transformation preserves or changes it, show intermediate steps and checks, and use fresh practice with targeted remedial retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 45,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13 “Ratio and Proportion,” printed pages 426–474. Approved scope: For a:b, distinguish the share a/(a+b) from the comparison a/b; convert each requested part to a percentage using its stated whole. Translate “A is p% of B,” “A exceeds B by p%,” and sequential percentage changes into equations before finding a ratio. Emphasize that “p% more than” reverses differently from “p% less than” because the base changes. Check a ratio-to-percent result by summing complementary parts to 100% and confirming direction. Use solved examples 16–18 and matching objective problems."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Ratios, Fractions, and Percentage Comparisons: Move between ratios, fractions of a whole, and percent comparisons without changing the base. This lesson builds the ratio model, works through its reasoning, then checks understanding with fresh practice and targeted recovery.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A ratio a:b compares the first amount with the second; the first amount’s fraction of their combined whole is a/(a+b). These are related but answer different questions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If female:male=16:9, the class has 25 equal parts and females make 16 of them. Thus females are 16/25×100%=64% of the class.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Convert a ratio to a percentage of the whole by adding its terms for the denominator. Do not divide the first term by the second unless asked for the first as a percentage of the second.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Translate “A is 40% more than B” as A=1.4B. Translate “B is 20% less than C” as B=0.8C. Chain the equations only after identifying each base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For B=0.8C, B:C=4:5. If A=1.4B, then A:C=1.4×0.8=1.12, so A:C=28:25. The percentages use different reference quantities.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A is 25% greater than B does not mean B is 25% less than A. If B=100, A=125; the reverse decrease is 25/125=20%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check complementary shares: if A:B=16:9, A’s class share is 64% and B’s is 36%; they add to 100%. A result over 100% signals a wrong denominator.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When a percentage is nested inside a ratio statement, convert it into a multiplier first. This keeps the algebra explicit and avoids mixing percentage points with ratios.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Fractions of a total are most useful for distributing a known whole; pairwise ratios are most useful for comparison. State which interpretation you are using before calculating.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If both quantities increase by the same percentage, their ratio is unchanged because both are multiplied by the same factor. Different percentage changes generally alter the ratio.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For reverse comparisons, solve from the new base. If A is 20% more than B, then A=1.2B and B/A=1/1.2=5/6; B is 16⅔% less than A.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use a bar model or 100-unit reference when comparing percentages. It makes the reference whole visible and is especially helpful when percentages are chained.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The direction of the result should match the language: if A exceeds B, the A term in A:B should be larger. This simple order check catches inversion errors.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "In mixed questions, first convert the relationship to equations, then reduce the ratio, then convert to a percent only if asked. Keep intermediate ratios exact.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "You should now be able to move between a ratio, a fraction of the whole, and percentage comparisons while naming each denominator and base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Decide which quantities form the ratio and keep their order visible. Use combined parts in the denominator, not only the second term. Use the given relationships before substituting numbers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
